<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#war-reparations">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">War reparations</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as reparations</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">War reparations are payments imposed on a defeated state to compensate the victors for the costs and damage of war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> of 188 BCE required the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> to pay 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> in reparations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> imposed heavy reparations on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> link to the economic and political crises of the post-war period.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most significantly, the war guilt clause of Versailles, Article 231, and the <b>reparations</b> it justified fuelled the resentments from which National Socialism later drew strength, making the German Empire's collapse arguably the most consequential imperial dissolution of the twentieth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945, war <b>reparations</b> to the Soviet Union, paid in ships, machinery and cables, stimulated engineering and metal industries, and bilateral trade with the Soviet Union became a major outlet for Finnish goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The ransom paid to France, estimated to have cost between $21 billion and $115 billion in lost growth, made Haiti the first post-colonial state crippled by debt; in 2013 its government called on European governments to pay <b>reparations</b> for slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Congress apologised and paid <b>reparations</b> in 1988.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-19">19 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mughals agreed to pay the equivalent of ₹5,000,000 in <b>reparations</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-7">7 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It accepted territorial losses, disarmament and <b>reparations</b>, which fuelled lasting resentment.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
